ngages POL-NS-09(a). The case is therefore escalated: no money is moved, and the disputed SGD 4.50 is held rather than collected or refunded.\n\nOn the surviving claims. The rider's POL-NS-06 shortfall argument (longest continuous run 145 s versus the 180 s minimum) and the driver's contrary POL-NS-01/POL-NS-12-style argument (a 400 s qualifying run, so the dwell is met) are answered together: the impaired trace cannot establish either, so neither can carry a payment or a refund. The fare-reconciliation claim is accepted - the SGD 4.50 cancellation-fee line equals platform_policy.cancellation_fee_sgd of SGD 4.50, so there is no POL-NS-08(d) fare defect - but it does not decide the dwell. The driver's POL-NS-07 compensation claim (SGD 3.60, i.e. 80% of SGD 4.50) depends on POL-NS-01 being satisfied in full; limb (c) is undecided, so no compensation issues now. The anti-farming check does not trip (driver_profile.no_show_terminations_30d 1, ratio 0.012), and no POL-NS-09(b), (c) or (d) trigger is engaged (0 counterparty disputes in 90 days, 0 no-show disputes in 30 days). POL-NS-04 cannot exonerate the rider: the chat log EV-CHAT-02732 (weight 1.0) carries 0 messages, so limb (a) is not met. POL-NS-11 makes the absent rider track (0 rider fixes) neutral rather than adverse, so it neither helps nor hurts the rider.\n\nOpen question for the human reviewer: whether the driver actually held the arrival radius continuously for the full 180-second free waiting period across the 195-second unobserved stretch between 17:07:36 and 17:10:51. A full-resolution GPS export covering that interval, or a platform-confirmed arrival and wait-timer record, would settle it."
